From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-fs: macos: keep guest names and symlinks inside the
 shared directory

The macOS passthrough server reaches an inode through "/.vol/{dev}/{ino}"
(which the kernel turns into the inode's real path) and appends the
name the guest sent. Names were not checked, so a guest that drives
the virtio-fs queue itself could send "..", or "a/b", and look up,
create, rename, unlink or link files anywhere the host user can, e.g.
~/Library/LaunchAgents. Path-based calls on an existing inode also
followed a symlink in the final component: open (and so truncate and
DAX mappings), chmod (setattr and the set_secctx fallback), the xattr
calls, statvfs, and link, which on macOS hard-links the symlink's
target. A symlink in the share (one the guest made, pointing anywhere)
therefore gave access to its target on the host.

Every name the guest sends (lookup, mkdir, mknod, create, symlink,
link, both rename names, unlink, rmdir) must now be a single directory
entry: empty, ".", ".." and names with '/' fail with EINVAL. The guest
kernel never sends such names. Operations on an existing inode no
longer follow symlinks: open uses O_NOFOLLOW (was explicitly removed),
chmod is fchmodat(AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW), path xattr calls pass
XATTR_NOFOLLOW, link is linkat() without AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW, and statfs
of a symlink fails with ELOOP. The guest kernel resolves symlinks
itself and never opens one through FUSE, so guests see no change.

The worker serves one request at a time, so a guest cannot swap a
directory for a symlink between building a path and using it.
---
 .../src/virtio/fs/macos/passthrough.rs        | 304 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 289 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/devices/src/virtio/fs/macos/passthrough.rs b/src/devices/src/virtio/fs/macos/passthrough.rs
index 16d5bbb..5c6df84 100644
--- a/src/devices/src/virtio/fs/macos/passthrough.rs
+++ b/src/devices/src/virtio/fs/macos/passthrough.rs
@@ -165,6 +165,19 @@ fn einval() -> io::Error {
     linux_error(io::Error::from_raw_os_error(libc::EINVAL))
 }
 
+// A name the guest sends must be exactly one directory entry of its parent. Inodes are reached
+// through "/.vol/{dev}/{ino}", which the host kernel turns into the real path of the inode, and
+// a name is appended to it, so "..", "." or a name with '/' would reach paths outside the shared
+// directory (or name the parent itself). The guest kernel never sends such names; only a guest
+// that drives the virtio queue directly does. A `CStr` cannot contain a NUL byte.
+fn check_name(name: &CStr) -> io::Result<()> {
+    let bytes = name.to_bytes();
+    if bytes.is_empty() || bytes == b"." || bytes == b".." || bytes.contains(&b'/') {
+        return Err(einval());
+    }
+    Ok(())
+}
+
 fn item_to_value(item: &[u8], radix: u32) -> Option<u32> {
     match std::str::from_utf8(item) {
         Ok(val) => match u32::from_str_radix(val, radix) {
@@ -363,7 +376,15 @@ fn set_host_stat(
 
     if let Some(mode) = mode {
         let res = match file {
-            InodeHandle::Path(path) => unsafe { libc::chmod(path.as_ptr(), mode as u16) },
+            // AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW: chmod(2) would change the mode of a symlink's target.
+            InodeHandle::Path(path) => unsafe {
+                libc::fchmodat(
+                    libc::AT_FDCWD,
+                    path.as_ptr(),
+                    mode as u16,
+                    libc::AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW,
+                )
+            },
             InodeHandle::Fd(fd) => unsafe { libc::fchmod(*fd, mode as u16) },
